The commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 58, verse 22:

"You will not find a people who believe in Allah and the Last Day loving those who oppose Allah and His Messenger, even if they were their fathers or their sons or their brothers or their kinsfolk." 

This noble verse comes in the form of a declarative statement (khabar), but what is meant is an imperative (insha'). This is a firm prohibition and a severe warning against allying with the enemies of Allah. Expressing an imperative in the form of a declarative is stronger and more emphatic than expressing it directly as an imperative, as is well known in its proper place. The meaning of His words, "they love those who oppose Allah and His Messenger," is: they love and befriend the enemies of Allah and His Messenger.

The prohibition and severe warning against befriending the enemies of Allah contained in this noble verse is clarified in other verses, such as:

- His words, Exalted is He: "There has already been for you an excellent pattern in Abraham and those with him, when they said to their people, 'Indeed, we are free of you and of what you worship other than Allah. We have disbelieved in you, and there has appeared between us and you enmity and hatred forever until you believe in Allah alone'" [al-Mumtahanah: 4];

- His words, Exalted is He: "Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and those with him are forceful against the disbelievers and merciful among themselves" [al-Fath: 29];

- His words, Exalted is He: "Allah will bring forth a people He loves and who love Him, humble toward the believers, powerful against the disbelievers" [al-Ma'idah: 54];

- His words, Exalted is He: "And let them find in you harshness" [al-Tawbah: 123];

- His words, Exalted is He: "O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be harsh upon them" [al-Tawbah: 73];

and other verses besides these.

As for His words in this noble verse, "even if they were their fathers," some have claimed that it was revealed concerning Abu 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, saying that he killed his father while he was a disbeliever on the day of Badr or the day of Uhud. It was also said that it was revealed concerning Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, the well-known hypocrite. Those who said this claimed that Abd Allah asked the Prophet ﷺ for permission to kill his father, Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, but the Prophet forbade him. It was also said that it was revealed concerning Abu Bakr. Those who said this claimed that his father, Abu Quhafah, insulted the Prophet ﷺ before his conversion to Islam, so his son Abu Bakr struck him until he fell.

(p-557) As for His words, "or their sons," some have claimed that it was revealed concerning Abu Bakr when he sought to engage his son 'Abd al-Rahman in single combat on the day of Badr.

As for His words, "or their brothers," some have claimed that it was revealed concerning Mus'ab ibn Umayr. They said: he killed his brother 'Ubayd ibn Umayr. Others said: he passed by his brother on the day of Badr, who was being taken captive by a man from the Muslims, and he said: "Tighten the bonds on him, for his mother is wealthy and she will ransom him."

As for His words, "or their kinsfolk," some said: it was revealed concerning 'Ubaydah ibn al-Harith ibn al-Muttalib, Hamzah ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, and 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them, when they killed 'Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn 'Utbah in single combat on the day of Badr. They were their paternal cousins, for they were the sons of Rabi'ah ibn 'Abd Shams ibn 'Abd Manaf, and 'Abd Shams was the brother of Hashim, as is not hidden.

As for His words, Exalted is He: "those – He has written faith into their hearts" [al-Mujadilah: 22], that is, He established it in their hearts by His grant of success.

What this noble verse contains regarding the establishment of faith in their hearts is clarified in His words, Exalted is He: "But Allah has endeared to you the faith and has made it beautiful in your hearts, and He has made hateful to you disbelief, defiance, and disobedience. Those are the rightly guided, by bounty from Allah and favor" [al-Hujurat: 7–8].
